<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Claims the domain Nook Plus issues handles under, which is what lets the
	     system offer to save a newly created account password and fill it back in
	     later. Without it the system refuses outright: there is no partial mode,
	     and nothing is saved under any name.

	     Applied to Debug as well as Release. It was Release-only for as long as the
	     project was signed by a personal team, because Associated Domains is not
	     available to one at all — Xcode refuses to create the profile, so carrying
	     it in Debug would have blocked everyday on-device development for no gain.
	     The team is enrolled now, so a development build behaves like a shipped one
	     and the save flow can be exercised without archiving.

	     Production only. Staging was listed alongside it while this file was being
	     worked out, with `?mode=developer` so the device would fetch the association
	     file straight from the host instead of through Apple's CDN — a mode that
	     needs Settings > Developer > Associated Domains Development switched on, and
	     so means nothing in a shipped build. It is gone: a developer-mode
	     association is not something to submit for review, and work that needs
	     staging can add it back locally without committing it.

	     One thing this does not cover: `nooker.social`, which is the host somebody
	     actually authenticates against. Credentials are scoped per domain, so a
	     password saved for nooker.app will not be offered on the PDS's own sign-in
	     page. That only matters if the app moves to the PDS's OAuth flow, and it is
	     the change to make first if it does. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>webcredentials:nooker.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
